---
/* THE CLOSE, WHICH IS NOW A PERSON RATHER THAN A PARAGRAPH.

   It carried three sentences saying there is no installer, no date, an open
   repository, a room and an address. Every one of those is on the page as an
   object: the heading says there is no release, the two buttons ARE the
   repository and the room, and the address is the line under them. The
   paragraph was the page reading its own closing section aloud.

   What is not anywhere else is who is behind it, and on a project with no
   release that is the more useful fact. So the paragraph is a quotation and a
   name.

   This does not break the repository's rule against quoting the owner. That
   rule governs the documentation set, where a quotation reads as the authority
   a record rests on, and it exists because a dictated brief is one instance
   stated under load. A signed line on a product site is the opposite artifact:
   it is written to be published, it is the owner's to approve, and it is
   attributed on purpose. */
import Acts from './Acts.astro';
---
{/* IT IS A DARK PLANE, LIKE THE TURN AND THE NUMBERS. The page steps down to
    `--bg-sidebar` where it wants the reader to stop moving, and this is the
    last of those places -- the section that says there is nothing to install
    and then asks for the one thing there is to do. It was the only one of the
    three drawn on the ordinary ground, which read as the page trailing off
    rather than closing. `.plane` also brings the ruled sheet up through it,
    so the ground the page opened on is the ground it ends on. */}
<section class="close plane">
  <div class="wrap close__in">
    <h2 class="rise">Nothing is released yet.<br />That is <em>the point</em>.</h2>

    <figure class="quote rise" data-d="1">
      <blockquote>
        <p>I never wanted a better ear. They all have an ear. What I kept missing is what
          happens after it: my words stay mine, in folders I own, and exactly one agent gets
          to touch them. It should be the best dictation app on the desk too. Give me a year.</p>
      </blockquote>
      {/* THE SIGNATURE IS THE NAME, AND THE NAME IS NOT THE ADDRESS. A line
          reading "Felix, who maintains this" set at eleven pixels is a
          footnote nobody finds, and it spends the reader's attention on the
          sentence rather than on the person. This is how a letter closes
          instead: the role in small type, the name signed under it, in the
          italic the page already loads.

          NO DOT IN THE SIGNATURE. What is drawn is `felixontv`, which is the
          name; what it points at is the domain. Signing a page with a URL is
          not signing it, and the dot is the one character that makes a reader
          parse the word as an address instead of as a person. The href is
          unchanged, so the link still goes where a reader would expect the
          name to take them.

          One line in the source per element, because Astro trims the
          indentation of the next one. */}
      <figcaption class="sig">
        <span class="sig__k mono">maintained by</span>
        <a class="sig__n" href="https://felixon.tv" rel="me">felixontv</a>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <Acts delay="2" />
    {/* A BUTTON, BECAUSE PRESSING IT DOES NOT NAVIGATE. It hands over the
        address rather than opening a client the reader may not have bound to
        the scheme; `src/lib/copy.ts` carries the derivation. */}
    <p class="close__mail rise" data-d="2">
      Or write to <button type="button" class="close__addr mono" data-copy="forge@sw-labs.de">forge@sw-labs.de</button>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
